    ":question/id": "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-9556",
    ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
