      ":question/id": "494f0061-8555-5747-be24-aea11c4501ae",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-126428",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\pi ,0).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\pi ,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(\\pi ,0)} & =-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-\\pi \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
