      ":question/id": "a254e595-25b7-5631-ae7f-2ec647939f75",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-168118",
      ":question/problem": "The scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $V$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $V$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
