      ":question/problem": "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({y}^{3}-x)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(-1,1).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-x)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-1-(-2y) \\\\  & =2y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
